{"title":"2010 USAJMO Problems","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Day_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Day 1</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Problem_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Problem_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Problem_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 3</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Day_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Day 2</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Problem_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Problem_5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 5</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Problem_6\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 6</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Day_1\">Day 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Day 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_1\">Problem 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>A permutation of the set of positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/c/e8c93faa47d8dd3b502efa51cb331e7d5b1fe340.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[n] = \\{1, 2, \\ldots, n\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"140\" height=\"19\" /> is a sequence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/6/d764605d3b0d0cde31b22bddb39c21e39615356b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"114\" height=\"18\" /> such that each element of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/e/0deff9086fd7840ef23860f5bc924f1d4d2d2310.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[n]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"19\" height=\"18\" /> appears precisely one time as a term of the sequence. For example, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/c/00cebb6c055baf3e5cac4cadf1ce09706e7051ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(3, 5, 1, 2, 4)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"90\" height=\"18\" /> is a permutation of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/2/c4216c69e041fca0904e8c5366d4480d1b2ca8b8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[5]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"17\" height=\"18\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/f/7dfd17dc4d3100462df726e8eb7f9262783429eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"37\" height=\"18\" /> be the number of permutations of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/e/0deff9086fd7840ef23860f5bc924f1d4d2d2310.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[n]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"19\" height=\"18\" /> for which <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/3/ac30d79cada4469ca31e5aa4370894791fc5da4c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ka_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"30\" height=\"17\" /> is a perfect square for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/5/4d5d6afdb30595e16e1700beba0d8f32a3d2db6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1\\leq k\\leq n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"78\" height=\"15\" />. Find with proof the smallest <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/f/7dfd17dc4d3100462df726e8eb7f9262783429eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"37\" height=\"18\" /> is a multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/4/054090e60a5dcb8bc33213620b3af9278f9a3e12.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2010$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"35\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2010 USAJMO Problems/Problem 1\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_2\">Problem 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/d/49d98ffe996c0f0d3b82b284cba2113439fefea9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"46\" height=\"15\" /> be an integer. Find, with proof, all sequences <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/a/c9a38578d1fff8c2964ee4908e479912d68e0641.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{n-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"118\" height=\"11\" /> of positive integers with the following three properties:\n</p>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:lower-alpha\">\n<li> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/7/317ce93cfbb32f26f6369310ca227c11536b2cc8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1 &lt; x_2 &lt; \\cdots &lt; x_{n-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"164\" height=\"12\" />;\n</li><li> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/1/7b1275279e3ab83e503b7f6a7fc3d424fdc651a0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_i + x_{n-i} = 2n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"116\" height=\"14\" /> for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/a/33a3278e98ca637e5d87181f4222a38af9916135.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i = 1, 2, \\ldots, n - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"138\" height=\"16\" />;\n</li><li> given any two indices <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/2/8122aa89ea6e80784c6513d22787ad86e36ad0cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"10\" height=\"17\" /> (not necessarily distinct) for which <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/9/159f5319d016a9c29f712e6812f67523371bffdb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_i + x_j &lt; 2n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"99\" height=\"16\" />, there is an index <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/8/168cbdc97337408ae58d6cd9e8b9af4834b1e634.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_i + x_j = x_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"97\" height=\"15\" />.\n</li></ol>\n<p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2010 USAJMO Problems/Problem 2\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_3\">Problem 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/7/ed7ad6b337cd6377243225f7ccee4ffb6f5e47e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AXYZB$\" width=\"72\" height=\"13\" /> be a convex pentagon inscribed in a semicircle of diameter <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />. Denote by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/f/c9ffd7d6a650eb62b9db7f0f6cb69e25206a27dc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P, Q, R, S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"76\" height=\"16\" /> the feet of the perpendiculars from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/5/ce58e4af225c93d08606c26554caaa5ae32edeba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> onto lines <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/6/e767930387b0409c051b72e49333e2a32c280a5d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AX, BX, AZ, BZ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"138\" height=\"16\" />, respectively. Prove that the acute angle formed by lines <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/b/7ab17967324fc2b12349c0610c6c256f4c829162.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PQ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/3/7631e00d1deb6ad3d55301906009f15d342e412a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$RS$\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" /> is half the size of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/b/02b8393068ae4d9f94f9789816cb62e054769259.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle XOZ$\" width=\"57\" height=\"12\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is the midpoint of segment <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2010 USAMO Problems/Problem 1\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Day_2\">Day 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Day 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_4\">Problem 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>A triangle is called a <i>parabolic triangle</i> if its vertices lie on a parabola <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/7/ca7ebfe4cb06e20c204fa1dc02267597aad658cd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = x^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"50\" height=\"18\" />. Prove that for every nonnegative integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, there is an odd number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and a parabolic triangle with vertices at three distinct points with integer coordinates with area <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/b/28b5b70146244990729b282fffd796f7266f3c9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2^nm)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"54\" height=\"19\" />.\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2010 USAJMO Problems/Problem 4\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_5\">Problem 5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Two permutations <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/4/894251593f07c61e283127a19e55e7cc633a9b80.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_{2010}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"117\" height=\"11\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/b/1abd19986285f1d4be05821bb97027004adee065.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_1, b_2, \\ldots, b_{2010}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"112\" height=\"16\" /> of the numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/6/8460937de0afdf8125118f5214d602354ebbe8c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1, 2, \\ldots, 2010$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"105\" height=\"17\" /> are said to intersect if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/4/6045dddd3ce9b6ca0c5b71d5818e66b6db41f66a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_k = b_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"57\" height=\"15\" /> for some value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> in the range <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/2/312cbd8a8766c766ac899fb32c841c285eb18018.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1\\leq k\\leq 2010$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"103\" height=\"15\" />. Show that there exist <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/2/2524e7efcb927db9f32af0f14bd63706b4aa9028.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1006$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"35\" height=\"13\" /> permutations\nof the numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/6/8460937de0afdf8125118f5214d602354ebbe8c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1, 2, \\ldots, 2010$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"105\" height=\"17\" /> such that any other such permutation is guaranteed to intersect at least one of these <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/2/2524e7efcb927db9f32af0f14bd63706b4aa9028.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1006$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"35\" height=\"13\" /> permutations.\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2010 USAJMO Problems/Problem 5\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_6\">Problem 6</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 6\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> be a triangle with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/4/ea494684dc60997279257d5a54e877a1fb28ca60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle A = 90^{\\circ}$\" width=\"74\" height=\"13\" />. Points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> lie on sides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />, respectively, such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/b/8abe9cd198b78eb776b95d375c73a0553fa224e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ABD = \\angle DBC$\" width=\"138\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/7/92795aed81f4bb5f45bd951633bfe47843d98778.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ACE = \\angle ECB$\" width=\"135\" height=\"13\" />. Segments <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/f/89fff82bb65d0215e49c8c91cb7c553da52205e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/c/13c970cbf5e2e7029089517d5e8efbb8cade6d8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> meet at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.  Determine whether or not it is possible for segments <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/6/bf6a00bfbcedc80aaea0eb0c13cadbb4f783036d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB, AC, BI, ID, CI, IE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"192\" height=\"16\" /> to all have integer lengths.\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_6\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2010 USAJMO Problems/Problem 6\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO\" title=\"2010 USAJMO\">2010 USAJMO</a></b> (<b><a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=176&amp;year=2010\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b>First USAJMO</b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_USAJMO_Problems\" title=\"2011 USAJMO Problems\">2011 USAJMO</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2010 USAJMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2010 USAJMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_3\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2010 USAJMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2010 USAJMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2010 USAJMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_6\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2010 USAJMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAJMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAJMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAJMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-c